WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.420
Hey, y'all.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.730
I'm JD Long, and I
thought I'd pick up

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.657
our lecture from where we left
it off last year at my session.

00:00:15.657 --> 00:00:17.490
Actually, it was a
little bit uncomfortable,

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.865
because I thought I was going
to come in here and totally

00:00:19.865 --> 00:00:21.360
troll the crap out of you guys.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.830
And because I'm going to talk
about Excel and spreadsheets,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.600
I got nothing left,
so I just made up

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.660
another presentation in the
last 5 seconds of the keynote.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.721
So let's just kind
of go together

00:00:32.721 --> 00:00:33.720
and see where this goes.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.580
Now, I'm Vice President
of Risk Analytics

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.930
at a global reinsurance company.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.420
And don't worry, nobody else
knows what that means either.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.880
And it was very confusing
for my daughter,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:45.990
when she was growing up.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.522
So I'd go to parent-teacher
conference and things.

00:00:48.522 --> 00:00:50.730
You've got to explain like,
oh, well, what do you do?

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.880
And so I just had
shirts printed up,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.140
because it made everything
easier to explain.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.830
So I'm very empathetic to
the role of spreadsheets.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.340
My whole life has been around
spreadsheets and programming.

00:01:07.451 --> 00:01:08.950
I've got to give a
quick disclaimer.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.540
I'm going to mention that
I work at RenaissanceRe,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.081
and I usually never mention it,
because the stock trades down

00:01:13.081 --> 00:01:15.770
every time I'm associated
with the company.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.130
I'm not giving financial
advice, and I'm not giving

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.560
forward-looking statements.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.750
My junk is on GitHub, though.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.220
Feel free to get
this presentation.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.040
Anything that went
into it will be there.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.154
So this was my great
trolling comment

00:01:29.154 --> 00:01:30.820
I was going to lead
with, that I thought

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:34.742
was going to be provocative
but kind of seems

00:01:34.742 --> 00:01:36.700
lame in comparison to
what we just sat through.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.210
So to migrate Excel
analysts to coding,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.550
help them be better
at Excel first.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:47.560
This is kind of what I'm
going to progress with today.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.570
And if you fail to migrate
them to R or anything else,

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.960
at least you'll have better
Excel, and that's not nothing.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.270
So I'm coming from
an environment

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:02.230
of a workplace that's
largely based around Excel.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:06.700
Now, I am suspicious the
business analyst work

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.100
environment is not that
different than analysts

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.360
in non-profit, or people
who work in maybe finance,

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.860
or other places, other
types of organizations.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.610
So I'm going to speak my
first-person experience, which

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.244
is around business, but I think
a lot of this is applicable.

00:02:22.244 --> 00:02:23.410
Some of you all may know me.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.410
I run my mouth on
Twitter way too much.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.350
Been involved in the art
community there for years.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.450
Paul Teetor, a friend
of mine-- he and I

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.900
are working on second edition
of this book for O'Reilly.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.450
We're in final
editing, coming soon

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.220
to fine booksellers near you.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.710
And no presentation
at this conference

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.800
would be complete without
an Allison Horst diagram.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.190
Now, unlike everyone else
who is lifting things

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.910
that Allison put on Twitter,
this was a custom piece.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:54.370
Allison and I discussed
what I was presenting,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.770
and she came up with this,
and I think it's fantastic.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.980
So corporations tend
to be sad silos.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.370
We've got our business user,
Clippy, and Excel, and Word,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.010
and they all go to the
same parties together.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:12.280
And GitHub is over here
with the codery type people.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.320
Jupyter's a little
concerned, because he's

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.030
been reading all
the crap on Twitter

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.830
about how notebooks
might not be a good idea,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.260
and he's a little nervous.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.070
And RMAN has beautiful
plots but is all

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.860
alone over in the corner.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.800
And I am here to tell you, it
doesn't have to be this way.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.660
So we can have a totally
different relationship

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.630
with these tools, but it's
not going to be the default.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.900
And I am convinced the reason
we end up with everybody

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.380
in their own little
silos is, largely, we're

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.350
tribal animals, right?

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.800
We look and we say,
oh, I am a coder.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.900
Therefore, I value using
these tools this certain way.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.170
And the way you're using the
tool looks illegitimate to me,

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.630
because you don't have unit
test, and you don't have this,

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.970
and you don't always use
version control the way

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.259
I would use version
control, and therefore, I'm

00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:07.300
more legitimate than you.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.220
And yes, totally what we just
listened to earlier today.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.240
I think we need to broaden
our tent a little bit.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.140
And this is very much the theme
we just heard in the keynote,

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.200
that we're using
some similar tools--

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.700
we, between programmers,
analysts, business analysts,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.440
people who use Excel only--

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.780
we're using some
different tools,

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.760
but there's some overlap.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.290
And I'm not going to be
using Python or R or any

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.380
of the coding tools
in quite the same way

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.650
that the professional
developers who I work with use.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:45.610
But I'm still using them,
and it's still production.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.370
And what's important for
us to think about is,

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.710
nobody wants to learn coding.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.160
All we want to do is,
we want to kick ass.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.270
And if you go into helping
other people learn a new tool

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:02.320
and your mindset is, I'm going
to use a pedagogical method

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.710
to profoundly change the way
they think-- nobody cares.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.440
Like, nobody cares.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.980
They want to be a superhero, and
it's important to think about,

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.510
how do analysts
think of themselves?

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.370
They're solving problems.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:19.870
They're doing heavy lifting.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.470
And they're doing it
all while it's on fire.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.410
And they are not going to take
the time to necessarily learn

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:28.962
a completely new
philosophy about how

00:05:28.962 --> 00:05:30.670
to think about the
world, because they're

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:34.180
too busy fighting fires
and wanting to kick ass.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.880
So if you approach
helping people get better

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.760
at what they're doing,
we need to think

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.280
about how we help them,
because we often don't exactly

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.140
help them get there
in a way that's

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.270
meaningful to where they are.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.510
Now, who in here has
heard of Kathy Sierra?

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:56.380
Anybody?

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.090
Because I'm totally
ripping off-- like,

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.270
the rest of my presentation
is Kathy Sierra rebranded.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.930
She was a developer advocate
before developer advocacy

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.250
was even a thing.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.590
She wrote a number
of books on Java--

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:11.620
comes from a Java background--
but all the way down

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.830
to the phrase "kicking ass"
comes from Kathy Sierra here,

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:18.190
and I'm just recirculating
it for our use case.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:21.340
But she has this wonderful
graph about learning,

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:25.000
and there's this suck threshold,
and what we're trying to get to

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.680
is the passion threshold.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.280
So what gets people feeling
passionate about any tool is,

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.620
they feel like they're
kicking ass with it.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.860
And the suck threshold
is when it is horrible,

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.080
nothing makes sense,
and we can't figure out

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:39.280
what to do next.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.950
And if we start
trying to teach--

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.652
especially to these firefighters
dead lifting flaming weights

00:06:45.652 --> 00:06:47.860
and is worrying about the
whole company coming down--

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.350
and if we spend
trying to teach them,

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:54.580
here's 5 different types of
objects you may encounter,

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.620
you're just moving
along the time axis,

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.560
and you aren't moving
them up the ability axis.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.280
So my key takeaway
here is, as you're

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:05.680
trying to talk to people
in your organization

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.660
about upping their
skill, whatever

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:13.060
the skill is, get them doing
things they perceive as useful.

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:16.480
Way more important than
learning a body and knowledge.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.640
Now, back to my whole OK,
help people migrate Excel

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.740
by helping them be
better at Excel.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:25.330
Let me give us some
specific examples,

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.630
because that's a bit oblique.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:34.660
This is Excel in Mac with the
dark theme, because I'm cool.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.580
Very hip.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.740
Tell my 11-year-old.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.330
OK, so we've got
this workbook called

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.700
Iris workbook, because we've
got to use Iris, right?

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.050
Even though it's Excel.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.870
I'm blending cultures here.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.280
We've got this Iris
workbook, and I've

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.570
introduced a sheet
called Iris sheet,

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:54.220
and it has a table
in it-- a named

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.660
table-- called Irish table.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.160
Now, the concept
here that I think

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.370
is very important for users
of Excel to begin to use

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:04.360
is this idea of a named table.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.040
Who in here is
familiar with this idea

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.180
of naming a table in Excel?

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:08.680
OK, cool.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.580
So a big chunk of people.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.570
I have noticed this is
used very inconsistently,

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:16.390
and sometimes analysts who
are experienced with Excel

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:17.830
don't even know this exist.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.770
And it's important to help them
understand the value of this,

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:24.790
because you can do really
neat things, like reference

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:28.660
columns in a function.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:30.980
That feels a little
bit like using

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.230
a programming language, right?

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:35.110
So this is my other
provocative idea

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:36.700
that's no longer
provocative that's,

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:40.570
we're learning programming
in a reactive functional

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:43.000
environment, and that's good.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.030
This isn't a problem.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:45.160
This is a good thing.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:49.450
And we're helping an analyst,
or a traditional Excel user,

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:53.710
begin to think about a data
frame, and named columns in it,

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:55.720
and doing operations on those.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.290
And this is a vectorized
operation, and it's super.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:03.380
They can then use these
things other places,

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:08.540
like use that table name
when they make a pivot table.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:13.757
This is a natural segue
into things we do and are.

00:09:13.757 --> 00:09:15.590
One of the other things
that's very tempting

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:18.840
to do when you know the tools
that we all know is to say,

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:20.870
oh, I'm going to
take your workflow,

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:23.230
and I'm going to help
you fix it, right?

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:24.560
And I'm going to help you--

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:26.690
we're going to just
do it all over in R.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:30.200
The philosophy that I have
adopted after failing miserably

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:33.050
at that and basically
being told to go sod off

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:38.810
is, help people take the hard
parts, the high friction parts,

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:40.970
and build some
automation around that.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.040
Don't feel like
we have to change

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.330
someone's life completely.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.790
Make an incremental change.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:50.060
We've started, in my team,
calling this the 10% better

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:54.020
approach rather than trying
to completely fix a workflow.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.840
Every time we do it, we
try to make it 10% better.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.350
We have made more progress
through the 10% improvement

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.560
approach than we have through
any number of projects that

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.500
were grandiose and,
ultimately, failed,

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.090
because the scope was too big.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:12.880
But I can make anything
I do 10% better.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.950
But that R environment, though.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:20.100
So one of the challenges
for new R users

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.600
is setting up the environment.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.060
So where I work, we've
taken it out of the system.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.560
We use JupyterLab lab anyway,
so we have a JupyterLab log in.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:29.670
Log someone in.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.980
Gives them the R kernel, right?

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:33.300
That's pretty familiar.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.330
And it authenticates the user,
mounts their drive space,

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.440
loads their scripts.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:41.400
But we've got an extra
menu option in ours,

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.310
because we just launch
our studio from here--

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.090
you're authenticated--
and a user

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:53.640
can play with R without having
to put a request into IT,

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.930
without having to work out
those database connections.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:10:58.770
It's just there.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:04.620
And the ability for low
friction play is important.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.130
Now, I agree-- it's not
enough to just have it

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:09.809
there and expect
users to gravitate

00:11:09.809 --> 00:11:11.100
and start doing things with it.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.710
But if they don't have
to ask permission-- like,

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:17.790
they don't have to get a
service desk ticket in order

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.830
to get a piece of
software installed--

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.920
the probability of them
playing, experimenting, doing

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.410
something goes way up, and
especially if you've already

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.830
got database connections
defined, or file paths,

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:32.200
or whatever makes sense
in your organization.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:38.070
Now, Excel can be a bit of the
vise grip locking plier tool,

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:38.730
right?

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:40.200
It's a fantastic tool.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.180
Does all kinds of things--

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.190
make dashboards with it.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.530
It's fantastic.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:50.400
We can put a user
interface on top of it.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:53.320
It's fantastic.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.690
And we use Excel, right,
in a lot of the same ways.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:59.270
But this is the assembly line.

00:11:59.270 --> 00:11:59.990
Obviously, right?

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:02.420
So this is a professional
production environment.

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.010
I can't tell from
the picture, but I

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:08.690
guarantee there's 25 pairs
of vise grip locking pliers

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:11.150
in this assembly floor, right?

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:12.980
The principle is, the tools--

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.220
it isn't, oh, professional
production tool versus not

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:17.860
a professional production tool.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.630
Vise grip pliers are used
in production, right?

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.960
They just aren't holding the
doors on with them, right?

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.180
They're being used for
very specific purposes.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:30.890
Let's give an
example of a workflow

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:34.606
that I have found helpful
in illustrating pulling data

00:12:34.606 --> 00:12:35.730
in and out of Excel, right?

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.580
Because remember, the objective
is not get someone out

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:38.420
of Excel.

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:40.370
It's, how do we make
their workflow easier?

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:43.910
Well, a common piece is,
do an ETL process in R,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.190
plop the data in an
Excel spreadsheet

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:49.100
where you're refreshing
existing data, right?

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.840
And a lot of the
tools kind of assume

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.720
you're exporting to
Excel from nothing,

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:55.850
but I have a world
where analysts

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.820
have built lots of good
something inside their Excel.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:08.150
So hey, let's open Excel SX,
and let's drop in our Iris data.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:12.140
We can do that with the
write table function.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.510
And the thing I would
like to point out

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.470
is the two lower parameters--
table style and table name.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:22.430
This is where we get
named tables in Excel,

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:23.390
by using this feature.

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:27.410
So if they have built, in their
spreadsheet, formulas already

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:29.360
referencing tables
and columns, as I

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:31.220
have been trying to
encourage them to do,

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.720
we can replace their data now.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.940
Best practice-- I don't
illustrate it here--

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:38.600
delete the first table out,
and then drop the new in.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:40.760
Because otherwise, if
you drop fewer rows

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:42.240
in than were
underneath it, you've

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.300
got bad data peeking
out the bottom.

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.170
Not good.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.210
So delete the first
out-- and that's

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:49.610
not shown in this workflow--
but then write it in.

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:52.197
Give it a name table, and
it is in their environment

00:13:52.197 --> 00:13:53.780
where they're used
to working with it.

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:55.790
And we have just made
their process hopefully,

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:58.490
at least, 10% less
painful, right?

00:13:58.490 --> 00:13:59.925
This is the entry joke, right?

00:13:59.925 --> 00:14:00.800
The first hit's free.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.330
I think, right now, Mike
Smith is giving a presentation

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:11.350
showing this exact same XKCD So
this is getting some legs here.

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:16.420
We had a logical fallacy,
in my team, for a long time.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:18.400
At least, I perceive it
as a logical fallacy.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.140
We looked at this,
and this is the whole,

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:25.240
if it takes a long time
to automate a task,

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:27.760
but it doesn't save you a
lot of time, don't do it.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.740
Kind of seems obvious.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:33.520
Well, this illustration-- like
so many things-- is a model.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:34.930
Like, that's a model of reality.

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:37.510
That isn't actually
reality, and it's wrong,

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:39.310
because all models are
wrong by definition.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:41.143
They're actually fairly
useful to the extent

00:14:41.143 --> 00:14:42.100
the assumptions hold.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.860
Let me tell you a few of
the assumptions that are

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.540
wrong with this image, right?

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.800
So one of the assumptions is,
all time is of equal value.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:55.780
So we can't take 5
hours to fix this,

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.580
because it doesn't
save us that much time.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:02.170
It doesn't save us 5 hours over
the course of some time period.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:06.510
The fallacy there, in my
world, is unequal time value.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:08.440
So right around the
end of the quarter,

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:11.410
we have a flurry of
reporting activity.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:15.280
And that time is
incredibly precious.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.620
August, however--
nobody's there.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:19.450
Who cares what we
do in August, right?

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.970
So we can take time
in August, and if we

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.730
can save a few
minutes or a few hours

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.810
during our very, very busy
period, that's a great return.

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.980
And it's because our time
isn't of equal value.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:36.370
The other thing is, the
current frequency of reporting

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:38.499
is what we would want
to do in the future.

00:15:38.499 --> 00:15:40.540
And we have found this
not to be the case at all.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.370
As we have automated
things, we discover

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:45.700
we run them more often, and we
want to run them more often.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.070
We want to get the
analysis that we thought

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:49.690
we only needed once a quarter.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:51.640
Oh, if we can get
that daily, well, that

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:53.320
might change how we do things.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:54.910
So it ends up not
being stationary.

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:01.870
All time spent doing
analytics is of equal utility.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:07.600
Some tasks are so awful and
tedious that your analysts

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.990
will quit and go work to
Facebook instead of doing them,

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:11.830
right?

00:16:11.830 --> 00:16:13.360
Don't make your
analysts run away.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:16.090
Help them automate
those manual processes,

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:17.720
not because they
take a long time,

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.210
but because they suck their joy.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:23.960
And the other assumption
that's fallacious

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:25.880
is that automated
and manual workflows

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:28.820
produce the same product.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.150
This is not the case.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:34.670
The product produced out
of an automated process

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:38.486
should be more accurate
if you're doing it right.

00:16:38.486 --> 00:16:40.610
And if it's not, it's at
least inaccurate in scale,

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.950
which is fantastic.

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:46.040
The idea of our
10% better approach

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:50.720
came from Dan Harris's book
10% Happier, about meditation.

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.570
And we used to
call it, actually,

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:55.070
our incremental
improvement process,

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:56.840
but then we had a different
project called Incremental,

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:58.340
and we can't have
a naming clash,

00:16:58.340 --> 00:16:59.720
and naming things is hard.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:03.770
So anyway, we ended up calling
this process 10% happier.

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:05.750
And we have found that
it is truly-- sorry,

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:09.680
10% improvement-- we found that
it has made us 10% happier.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:13.880
And we're more time, in this
dynamic environment, where

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:18.410
our tools are getting along,
and the people aren't in silos.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:22.430
And this is, obviously,
not where we live everyday,

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:27.140
but it's the state that we're
aspiring to be and more.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.125
So with that, I'll wrap up.

00:17:29.125 --> 00:17:30.500
I want to point
out, the art here

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.330
is by the wonderful
Allison Horst.

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.070
Shout at her on Twitter.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:36.600
She's fantastic, and
wonderful, and did these.

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:38.900
We dialogued only
online, and she

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.580
came up with this
wonderful artwork,

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:41.750
so I've got to thank her.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:46.820
Now, I've got a minute
for some questions.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:47.320
Let's see.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:49.798
We've got mics.

00:17:49.798 --> 00:17:53.690
[INAUDIBLE]

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:54.695
Oh, go for it.

00:17:54.695 --> 00:17:58.580
Where could I get that shirt?

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:01.124
So I had this custom
made, and I realized,

00:18:01.124 --> 00:18:02.540
as I was coming
up here, I'm like,

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.040
oh, I guess I should
have done, like,

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.160
an Etsy store or something,
because I kind of think

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:07.118
that this may resonate.

00:18:07.118 --> 00:18:09.660
This may be other people's
love language as well as mine.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.158
We've got other questions?

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:19.334
Right back here.

00:18:19.334 --> 00:18:22.250
Do you have other resources
that you utilize for how

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:24.680
to kind of [INAUDIBLE]?

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:30.250
Yeah, that's a good idea.

00:18:30.250 --> 00:18:33.280
The question was, do I have
other tips or resources?

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.620
I'm afraid that I
don't, and the ones I do

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:39.580
have are super focused on
the workflows that we do.

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:41.620
But I think this is a
neat idea, and if someone

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:44.464
would put together an
online resource of this--

00:18:44.464 --> 00:18:45.880
and I'll give some
thought to it--

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:47.500
I think that would be fantastic.

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:50.020
Because I'm sure there
are design patterns

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:53.110
that get repeated over and over,
like my example of popping data

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:54.490
out and popping it in.

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:58.210
I'm suspicious that's a
common design pattern.

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.520
But sorry, I don't
have anything.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:02.160
All right.

